From 911ff8efeac69dafd775c14889faef93da036182 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:44:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/18] BananaDS perf: the 2D compositor's step loop unrolled
 by a fold, for the device's -O2 (render.2d_comp_unroll)

BananaLinux builds BananaDS at -O2 (Buildroot's flags), not the -O3 of the PC builds. CompositeLoop, the
painter's pass over the layer lines four pixels at a time, keeps its per-step constants in small arrays and
loops over the steps for every four pixels; at -O3 GCC unrolls that loop and keeps the arrays in registers, at
-O2 it reloads them from the stack for every step: 2.5-3x the instructions per line (device compiler, under
qemu: 2 BG steps 2588 vs 847, 3 steps with the second line 3966 vs 1432, 4 steps with an OBJ priority line and the
3D line 5665 vs 1833; core's qemu-count of membench: Composite 248k -> 711k instructions a frame). The same loop
with the steps unrolled by a fold over an index sequence, each step's constants a variable of its own, costs at
-O2 what the loop costs at -O3 (1558 / 1833 / 2154), identical output. render.2d_comp_unroll=off keeps the plain
loop. The same operations in the same order (a fold goes left to right): the same pixels.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PvbFhKAw3ERk5a13S651g1
